# INPLAY-COLLECTOR on the VPS (VPS-CONSOLIDATION-2026-09-16 phase 5, 2026-09-22).
#
# The Epicbet in-play board collector + the two LOCKED slow-state paper bots.
# Moved off the operator's Mac once the residential egress existed.
#
# WHY IT COULD NOT MOVE BEFORE, AND WHAT CHANGED. Epicbet answers this box with
# HTTP 403 CF-CHALLENGE — on the LIVE endpoint too, not only pre-match — so every
# call would have gone through FlareSolverr. FS was already dropping ~15% of the
# pre-match Epicbet runs (EPICBET-FS-500, 7 of 48 measured 2026-09-21) and this
# loop adds ~33 calls/min on top; moving it onto that FS would have degraded a
# working feed on a placeable book. RESIDENTIAL-EGRESS removed the premise: via
# the WireGuard tunnel this box reaches Epicbet with PLAIN REQUESTS, no FS, no
# session budget, no contention. Verified: same fixture ids, same markets and
# identical prices as the Mac, fetched in the same minute.
#
# ⚠️ EXACTLY ONE COLLECTOR MAY RUN.
# `inplay_book_quotes` has NO unique constraint — only a PK on an autoincrement
# id — so a second collector does not conflict, it DUPLICATES: two rows per
# (fixture, instant), and the paper bots double-count into `shadow_bets` against
# a 3,000-pick target. Nothing errors; the experiment just stops meaning
# anything. Before starting this, confirm the Mac job is gone:
#     launchctl list | grep inplay-collector      # must print nothing
#
# NO FALLBACK BY DESIGN. If the tunnel is down the Epicbet client RAISES rather
# than quietly using the Hetzner IP. Restart=always + RestartSec=30 means this
# crash-loops slowly while the tunnel is down and self-heals when it returns —
# which is correct: collecting from the wrong network identity while reporting
# success is how EPICBET-403-FROM-VPS ran "fine" for six days writing zero rows.
#
# Install:
#   cp local/systemd/oddsintel-inplay-collector.service /etc/systemd/system/
#   systemctl daemon-reload
#   systemctl enable --now oddsintel-inplay-collector
#
# Manage:
#   systemctl status oddsintel-inplay-collector
#   journalctl -u oddsintel-inplay-collector -f

[Unit]
Description=OddsIntel Epicbet in-play collector (via residential egress)
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=root
WorkingDirectory=/opt/odds-intel-engine
EnvironmentFile=/opt/odds-intel-engine/.env

Environment=TZ=UTC
Environment=PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1
# The whole point of this unit. socks5h so DNS resolves AT the proxy.
# EPICBET-specific on purpose: the scheduler runs all 82 jobs in one process, so
# the shared OI_RESIDENTIAL_PROXY would flip Coolbet's transport at the same
# moment. Coolbet feeds the real-money path and moves on its own schedule.
# API-Football keeps the fast Hetzner path either way.
# ZONE-EGRESS 2026-09-23: was socks5h://10.8.0.2:1080, the WireGuard tunnel to
# the operator's MacBook. Now the zone.ee exit node (AS49604, Tallinn), which
# does the same job without depending on a laptop being awake. Measured: an
# Estonian HOSTING ip is classified like an Estonian eyeball ip — Epicbet goes
# CF-CHALLENGE -> OK. socks5h so DNS resolves at the exit, not here.
Environment=EPICBET_RESIDENTIAL_PROXY=socks5h://127.0.0.1:1081

ExecStart=/opt/odds-intel-engine/venv/bin/python -m workers.jobs.inplay_collector \
    --cadence 45 --max-fixtures 60

Restart=always
RestartSec=30

StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
